# Longhorn disk-headroom settings for the EXISTING tornis production cluster.
#
# ⚠️  OPERATOR-GATED. NOT applied by anything: not by CI, not by
# `kubectl apply -f deploy/ci/`, not by src/bootstrap.rs. The `.yaml.example`
# suffix keeps it out of every directory apply on purpose. Applying it is a
# deliberate, sequenced act — read the ORDERING section before you do.
#
# deploy/bootstrap/longhorn.yaml fixes NEW installs. It cannot fix this
# cluster: Longhorn here was installed from a Helm release (v1.11.1) that the
# repo does not own, and install-time defaults are ignored once the Setting
# objects already exist. Changing live values means writing the Setting CRs
# below.
#
# ── What is actually wrong (measured 2026-08-05, read-only) ────────────────
#
#   setting                                       live   upstream   effect
#   storage-over-provisioning-percentage           200        100    2x thin
#   storage-minimal-available-percentage            10         25    late stop
#   storage-reserved-percentage-for-default-disk    30         30    ok
#
# Both loosened values came from an imperative patch a human ran once and
# nobody recorded — the 200% line was literally an instruction in
# deploy/ci/README.md (removed by this MR). Their combined effect:
#
#   per node: 147.4 GiB disk - 44.2 GiB reserved = 103.2 GiB schedulable
#   ceiling at 200%:                              206.4 GiB
#   actually scheduled:                           205.0 GiB   (99.3% of ceiling)
#
# The cluster is one replica away from its own ceiling on every node. vm3 then
# reached 1.66 GiB available — about 9x BELOW the 14.7 GiB that the 10%
# minimal-available floor was supposed to protect, because that floor only
# gates NEW scheduling and does nothing about growth of replicas already
# placed.
#
# ── ORDERING — this is the part that matters ──────────────────────────────
#
# DO NOT APPLY WHILE vm3 IS FULL. Dropping over-provisioning 200 -> 100 while
# 205 GiB is scheduled against a new 103 GiB ceiling marks every disk
# unschedulable at once. It deletes nothing — Longhorn does not evict replicas
# to satisfy a lowered ceiling — but it blocks the replica REBUILDS that
# recovery depends on, which is exactly the wrong moment to take that away.
#
# Correct sequence:
#   1. Operator: give vm3 headroom (attach a data disk, as vm1 got 2026-08-03),
#      or reclaim the ~60 GiB of Prometheus PVC snapshots + the leaked replica
#      directory after a per-volume redundancy check.
#      -> org/runbooks/node-disk-pressure.md. This is issue #107's operator item.
#   2. Confirm every volume is `healthy` (none `degraded`/`unknown`) and all
#      three nodes are Schedulable.
#   3. Apply THIS file.
#   4. Re-check: no volume should become `degraded`, no PVC should go Pending.
#
# ROLLBACK: one command per setting, values are the current live ones —
#   kubectl -n longhorn-system patch settings.longhorn.io \
#     storage-over-provisioning-percentage --type=merge -p '{"value":"200"}'
#   kubectl -n longhorn-system patch settings.longhorn.io \
#     storage-minimal-available-percentage --type=merge -p '{"value":"10"}'
# Takes effect immediately; no restart, no data movement.
#
# NOTE ON 100%: with over-provisioning at 100 the cluster may not have room for
# every current volume's nominal size. That is the honest position — the
# capacity was never really there; 200% only hid it. If step 4 shows PVCs
# stuck Pending, the answer is more disk, not a higher multiplier.
---
apiVersion: longhorn.io/v1beta2
kind: Setting
metadata: { name: storage-over-provisioning-percentage, namespace: longhorn-system }
value: "100"
---
apiVersion: longhorn.io/v1beta2
kind: Setting
metadata: { name: storage-minimal-available-percentage, namespace: longhorn-system }
value: "25"
---
# Already 30 live; written so the value is recorded rather than assumed.
apiVersion: longhorn.io/v1beta2
kind: Setting
metadata: { name: storage-reserved-percentage-for-default-disk, namespace: longhorn-system }
value: "30"
